    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>"Water damage" and "flood damage" are different insurance categories. Standard homeowners and commercial policies generally exclude flood.</li>
<li>Sudden and accidental water, such as a burst pipe, is often covered; continuous or repeated seepage over time often is not.</li>
<li>Rain damage inside a building is commonly covered only when wind or hail first creates an opening in the roof or walls.</li>
<li>Flood coverage comes from the NFIP or a private flood policy, which usually has a 30-day waiting period for NFIP coverage.</li>
<li>Hidalgo County has dozens of flash flood and flood entries in NOAA's Storm Events Database, and mixed-cause claims after tropical systems are common.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Wet drywall looks the same whether the water came from a broken supply line or from a flooded street. Your insurance company will not see it that way. Understanding water damage vs. flood damage is one of the most important things a McAllen property owner can learn before the next heavy rain, because the source of the water often decides which policy responds, and whether any policy responds at all.</p>

<p>This guide explains how homeowners and commercial property policies typically treat water damage, how flood insurance works, what "sudden and accidental" means in practice, and how to handle claims where wind, rain, and rising water all arrive in the same storm. It is general information. Your policy wording controls.</p>

<h2>Water damage vs. flood damage: the basic difference</h2>

<p>In insurance terms, the question is usually not how much water there was, but where it came from and how it got in.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Water damage</strong> generally refers to water that originates inside the building or enters from above: a burst pipe, a failed water heater, an overflowing appliance, or rain that enters through a storm-damaged roof.</li>
<li><strong>Flood damage</strong> generally refers to surface water that rises and enters from outside and below. The NFIP and FEMA define a flood as a general condition where two or more properties or acres of normally dry land are partially or completely submerged by water from overflowing inland or tidal waters, unusual and rapid surface water runoff, mudflows, or related causes.</li>
</ul>

<p>That flood definition matters. Under the NFIP, a single home with water pooling in the yard may not meet it, while a street-wide flash flood after a slow-moving storm usually will.</p>

<h2>Comparison table: how coverage typically lines up</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Usually evaluated under</th><th>Common issues</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Burst supply line or water heater failure</td><td>Homeowners or commercial property policy</td><td>Sudden vs. long-term leak; cost to access the pipe; mold limits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain enters through a roof opening created by wind or hail</td><td>Homeowners or commercial property policy</td><td>Proving the storm created the opening; date of loss; prior roof condition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain enters through an old, worn roof with no new storm opening</td><td>Often excluded or limited under property policy</td><td>Wear and tear, maintenance, and deterioration exclusions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Slow leak under a sink or behind a shower over weeks or months</td><td>Often excluded as continuous or repeated seepage</td><td>Timeline of the leak; hidden vs. visible damage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sewer or drain backup</td><td>Property policy only if a backup endorsement applies</td><td>Endorsement limits; distinguishing backup from flood</td></tr>
<tr><td>Street or yard flooding enters the home or business</td><td>NFIP or private flood policy</td><td>Waiting periods; building vs. contents limits; proof of loss deadlines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane with roof damage and rising water</td><td>Both: property policy for wind/rain-through-opening, flood policy for rising water</td><td>Allocating damage between policies; separate deductibles</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>How homeowners policies treat water damage</h2>

<h3>Sudden and accidental vs. continuous seepage</h3>

<p>Most homeowners and commercial property policies cover water damage that is sudden and accidental. A washing machine hose that bursts and floods a laundry room is the classic example. The policy may also pay to tear out and replace part of a wall or slab to reach the broken pipe, though the pipe itself may or may not be covered depending on the form.</p>

<p>The flip side is the exclusion for continuous or repeated seepage or leakage. Many policies exclude damage from a leak that went on over a period of weeks, months, or years. Some forms specify a period, such as 14 days or more. Hidden leaks are a gray area: a leak inside a wall that the owner could not have seen may be handled differently from a dripping faucet ignored for a year. This is where policy wording and facts matter most.</p>

<p>McAllen's common construction affects these claims. Most homes here are slab-on-grade, so supply lines and drain lines often run in or under the slab. A slab leak can wet flooring and baseboards across several rooms before anyone notices, which raises questions about timing. Moisture readings, plumber reports, and photos taken at discovery all help establish what happened and when.</p>

<h3>Rain damage and the storm-created opening rule</h3>

<p>Rain is not the same as flood, but it is not automatically covered either. Many policies cover interior rain damage only when wind or hail first creates an opening in the roof or walls through which the rain enters. If the rain simply found its way through an aging roof, the claim may be denied under wear-and-tear exclusions.</p>

<p>That is why documenting the opening is essential after a storm. Photograph missing shingles, lifted flashing, broken skylights, damaged vents, and cracked or broken windows, and connect them to the date of the storm. NOAA's Storm Events Database can help confirm that damaging wind or hail occurred near your property on that date. Our <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage claims</a> pages explain what adjusters typically look for.</p>

<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Freeze-related pipe bursts</span><p>The February 2021 freeze brought temperatures in the lower 20s to upper teens across southern Hidalgo County, with McAllen International reporting a low of 21°F. Burst pipes from a freeze are usually a water damage question under the property policy, not a flood question. Policies may include conditions about maintaining heat or draining systems. See our guide to <a href="/freeze-damage-burst-pipe-claims-rio-grande-valley/">freeze damage and burst pipe claims</a>.</p></div>

<h2>How flood insurance works</h2>

<p>Because standard property policies generally exclude flood, McAllen owners who want flood coverage typically buy it separately through the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) or a private flood insurer.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Waiting period.</strong> According to FloodSmart, NFIP coverage generally takes effect 30 days after purchase, with limited exceptions such as coverage bought when making, increasing, extending, or renewing a mortgage.</li>
<li><strong>Building and contents are separate.</strong> NFIP policies have separate building and contents coverage. Standard NFIP residential limits are up to $250,000 for the building and $100,000 for contents. Renters can buy contents coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Deadlines are strict.</strong> NFIP policies have their own proof of loss requirements and deadlines, which are generally shorter and more rigid than many homeowners policies. Read your flood policy as soon as a loss happens.</li>
<li><strong>Private flood policies vary.</strong> Some private policies offer higher limits or additional living expense coverage. Others have different exclusions. Compare the actual wording.</li>
</ul>

<p>Flood zone maps matter for pricing and lender requirements, but flooding happens outside high-risk zones too. Poor drainage, intense short-duration rainfall, and urban runoff can flood streets and low-lying lots well away from mapped floodplains.</p>

