<h2>Flash flooding history in McAllen and Hidalgo County</h2>

<p>Water claims in the Rio Grande Valley (RGV) are not only a hurricane issue. NOAA's NCEI Storm Events Database lists 70 flash flood entries, 46 flood entries, and 30 heavy rain entries for Hidalgo County and its forecast zones. These entries cover reported events, not every storm that caused local street flooding, so the real history of water problems is broader than the database alone.</p>

<p>A few documented episodes show the range:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th><th>What NOAA records describe</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>July 16, 2007</td><td>EF1 tornado and heavy rain in McAllen</td><td>Intense thunderstorms produced a tornado along with torrential rainfall and widespread street flooding</td></tr>
<tr><td>July 23, 2008</td><td>Hurricane Dolly</td><td>Widespread minor to moderate structural damage across the Lower RGV and copious rainfall, with flooding primarily in low-lying and poor-drainage locations</td></tr>
<tr><td>July 25–26, 2020</td><td>Hurricane Hanna</td><td>Category 1 landfall on Padre Island (Kenedy County); tropical-storm impacts and major rainfall flooding in Hidalgo County</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Hurricane Hanna is a good example of why the water damage vs. flood damage question matters here. Hanna made landfall well up the coast in Kenedy County, yet NOAA records describe major rainfall flooding in Hidalgo County. When a storm's biggest impact inland is rain rather than wind, a property can end up with water inside from rising surface water and little visible roof damage. Without flood coverage, that kind of loss may fall outside a standard homeowners policy. Read more in our <a href="/hurricane-damage-mcallen-homeowners-guide/">hurricane damage guide for McAllen homeowners</a> and on our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page.</p>

<h2>Mixed-cause claims: when wind, rain, and flood arrive together</h2>

<p>Tropical systems and severe thunderstorms often bring more than one peril at once. A single storm can tear shingles off a roof, drive rain through the opening, and flood the street hard enough that water enters through the front door. That leaves the owner with potentially two claims under two policies, often with two adjusters and two deductibles.</p>

<h3>Why mixed-cause claims get complicated</h3>

<ul>
<li>The property insurer may attribute interior damage to flood, which its policy excludes.</li>
<li>The flood insurer may attribute damage to wind or rain, which its policy does not cover.</li>
<li>Some damage, such as wet drywall at mid-wall height, could plausibly come from either source.</li>
<li>Policy language, including anti-concurrent causation wording in some forms, can affect how mixed causes are handled.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How to document a mixed-cause loss</h3>

<ol>
<li><strong>Mark and photograph the flood line.</strong> Water lines on walls, cabinets, and doors show how high rising water reached. Measure from the floor.</li>
<li><strong>Document ceiling and upper-wall damage separately.</strong> Stains and wet insulation above the flood line point toward roof or wall openings.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph the roof and exterior openings.</strong> Missing shingles, displaced flashing, and broken windows help connect interior damage to wind.</li>
<li><strong>Record timing.</strong> Note when you first saw water coming through the ceiling versus when water entered at floor level.</li>
<li><strong>Keep mitigation records.</strong> Moisture maps, drying logs, and invoices from water mitigation companies often show where moisture was found and at what heights.</li>
<li><strong>Pull weather records.</strong> Rainfall and wind data for your date of loss help support the cause. Our <a href="/noaa-weather-records-document-property-damage-mcallen/">guide to using NOAA weather records</a> explains how.</li>
</ol>

<p>Report to both insurers promptly if you have both coverages. Do not assume one insurer will notify the other.</p>

<h2>Practical steps after any water loss</h2>

<ul>
<li>Stop the source if you can safely do so, such as shutting off the main water valve.</li>
<li>Take photos and video before moving anything, then mitigate: extract water, remove wet materials as needed, and run drying equipment.</li>
<li>Keep damaged materials or samples when practical, and photograph anything that must be thrown away.</li>
<li>Notify your insurer promptly and ask which policy and adjuster will handle each part of the loss.</li>
<li>Keep receipts for emergency services, hotel stays, and supplies.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists what to gather. If your water claim has been denied as "seepage," "flood," or "wear and tear," our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page explains how a licensed Texas public adjuster can review the policy language and the evidence. Coverage always depends on the policy and the facts, and questions about legal rights should go to a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>

<p>In McAllen, the line between water damage and flood damage is drawn by the source of the water, not the size of the mess. Know what your homeowners or commercial policy covers, consider whether flood insurance makes sense for your property before storm season, and document every water loss in a way that shows where the water came from and when.</p>
